00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:07.030
So this sample, it’s a very, very interesting
sample; it’s arsenic. So this is quality

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:08.030
arsenic.

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:16.289
So arsenic used to much more common in the
community than it is nowadays. It was used

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:24.949
as a pigment in the nineteenth century for
green wallpaper contained copper arsenite,

00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:30.949
because there weren’t any other good green
pigments to make the wallpaper. And when the

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:38.030
rooms were damp, Victorians didn’t like
opening the windows in their rooms, then mould

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:45.520
would grow on the wall. And because arsenic
is poisonous to most life forms the mould

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:54.230
would convert the arsenic into a volatile
compound, trimethylarsine, and the trimethylarsine

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:59.469
went into the air and several people were
killed by the arsenic coming from their wall

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:00.469
paper.

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:03.700
Now arsenic as we know is very toxic and there
have been a number of very high profile and

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:09.439
very famous cases of people that have poisoned
perhaps their partners or their business partners

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:17.410
using arsenic. Okay, so I’m going to very
carefully take this vial out. He says very

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:21.570
carefully then knocks it really hard.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:28.690
Arsenic was also used quite widely by people
to dispose of husbands, wives, lovers. Though

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:38.210
now it is much less common. It is used in
the electronics industry for getting electronic

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:49.600
properties for making transistors. And also
it is still sometimes used for medication

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.290
for feeding to livestock. It is only quite
recently that the use of arsenic has been

00:01:55.290 --> 00:02:00.850
banned as an additive to chicken feed in the
United States.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:07.930
So here you see the arsenic powder okay. Now
this is a metallic very dark metal, but I’m

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:12.380
not going to open this because like I said
it’s very, very toxic. It’s a very dark

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:17.320
metallic sample, you can see a very fine powder
in the bottom of this vial. Now I’m going

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.270
